- You can insert a new line with another way, is with the "endl" manipulator

Example:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
  cout << "Hello World!" << endl;
  cout << "I am learning About C++";
  return 0;
}

// Output will be
  Hello World
  I am learning about C++

Explanation:
- endl: This is a command (short for "end line"). When you put it in your code, it tells the computer to stop the current line and start a fresh one.
- Instead of writing \n to start a new line, you would write endl
  ^ Example with \n: cout << "Hello\nWorld";
  ^ Example with endl: cout << "Hello" << endl << "World";
  = Both result in: 
    Hello
    World
- Why use endl? While \n is faster in some high-performance scenarios, 
  endl is often preferred by beginners or in simple programs because it is more readable and ensures that the text is sent to the display immediately, 
  rather than waiting in a buffer
